[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Unity Architecture & The Editor"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mastering Unity requires a solid understanding of both its core architecture and how to effectively navigate and customize the Editor. This tutorial explores the foundational concepts of Scenes, GameObjects, Components, and Editor scripting."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Core Architecture: Entities and Components"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unity follows a compositional architecture rather than a deep inheritance hierarchy. The fundamental building block is the GameObject, which acts as a container. Functionality is then added by attaching Components to these GameObjects."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scenes: Containers for your game environment, acting as separate levels or screens."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GameObjects: The fundamental entities in Unity that represent characters, props, and scenery."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Components: Specialized scripts and modules (like rigidbodies, colliders, and custom scripts) that dictate GameObject behavior."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Writing a Custom Component"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"All custom behavior in Unity is derived from the MonoBehaviour class. Below is a standard architectural template for a managed game loop component."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5.0f;

    private void Update()
    {
        float moveHorizontal = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        float moveVertical = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        Vector3 movement = new Vector3(moveHorizontal, 0.0f, moveVertical);
        transform.Translate(movement * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime, Space.World);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Extending the Unity Editor"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of Unity's greatest strengths is the extensibility of its Editor. You can create custom inspectors and editor windows to streamline your workflow and enforce data validation."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;
using UnityEditor;

[CustomEditor(typeof(PlayerController))]
public class PlayerControllerEditor : Editor
{
    public override void OnInspectorGUI()
    {
        DrawDefaultInspector();

        PlayerController controller = (PlayerController)target;
        if (GUILayout.Button("Reset Position"))
        {
            Undo.RecordObject(controller.transform, "Reset Position");
            controller.transform.position = Vector3.zero;
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding how GameObjects, Components, and Editor scripts interact, you can build modular, maintainable game systems and highly efficient development pipelines in Unity."}]
